.TH SQLITE3 1
.SH NAME
sqlite3 \- execute SQL on a SQLite database
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sqlite3
[
.B -hr
]
[
.B -s
.I separator
]
.I database
[
.I sql
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sqlite3
executes the SQL argument, or reads SQL from standard input.
Complete statements are executed at line boundaries;
remaining input is executed at EOF.
Queries may span lines and contain multiple statements.
SQL errors stop execution and produce a non-empty exit status.
There are no dot commands or interactive prompts.
.PP
Each result row is written on one line, with columns separated by tabs.
.B -s
sets the separator and
.B -h
prints column names before each query's first row.
Values use SQLite's text conversion; NULL is empty.
Separators, newlines and binary data in values are not escaped.
Use SQL functions such as
.B hex
or
.B json_object
when an unambiguous representation is needed.
.PP
.B -r
opens an existing database read-only.
The name
.B :memory:
creates an in-memory database.
SQLite
.B file:
URIs are accepted.
.PP
Disk databases require the exclusive-use and synchronization semantics
described in
.IR sqlite3 (2).
New database files have
.B DMEXCL
set; an imported database needs
.BR "chmod +l" .
The file remains exclusively open until the command exits.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
sqlite3 :memory: 'select sqlite_version(), 6*7;'
sqlite3 /n/data/catalog.db <schema.sql
sqlite3 -h /n/data/catalog.db 'select * from items;'
sqlite3 -r 'file:/tmp/snapshot.db?immutable=1' 'pragma integrity_check;'
.EE
The last example requires a snapshot that will not change while open.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/sqlite3
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sqlite3 (2),
.IR chmod (1),
.IR gefs (4),
.IR ramfs (4)
